menu "Gateway: firmware updates (FW-1)"

    config GW_FW_CONFIRM_TIMEOUT_S
        int "Seconds a new firmware has to reach its first Sparkplug session"
        range 10 86400
        default 600
        help
            A new image boots on trial. Its first Sparkplug session (NBIRTH and every
            DBIRTH published) confirms it; without one within this time it is rolled back
            to the previous image, whatever the cause (also a broker that is down: the
            update can then be repeated). A reset during the trial (crash, watchdog,
            power cut) makes the bootloader roll it back, and an image that rejects the
            stored configuration is rolled back at once.

    config GW_FW_HTTP_TIMEOUT_MS
        int "Network timeout of a firmware download (ms)"
        range 1000 60000
        default 10000

    config GW_FW_TASK_STACK
        int "Stack of the download task (bytes)"
        range 6144 16384
        default 8192
        help
            The task exists only while a download runs (TLS handshake included).

    config GW_FW_TASK_PRIORITY
        int "Priority of the download task"
        range 1 7
        default 2

    config GW_FW_TRIAL_TASK_STACK
        int "Stack of the trial task of a new image (bytes)"
        range 4096 16384
        default 6144
        help
            Issue #108: a new image on trial has a task (core 0) for what its trial timer
            asks for: the rollback at the timeout, which verifies the previous image (its
            signature too, with signed images), and the retries of a failed confirmation.
            The esp_timer task (3.5 KB) overflowed with it. The task exists only while the
            image is on trial; the emulator build logs the stack the verification leaves.

    config GW_FW_TRIAL_TASK_PRIORITY
        int "Priority of the trial task"
        range 1 20
        default 5

    config GW_FW_PARK_OTHER_CORE
        bool "Park the other core while an image is verified (QEMU only)"
        depends on GW_IO_SIMULATED
        default n
        help
            Espressif's QEMU does not model the ESP32-S3 cache freeze that flash mappings
            use: code the other core runs from flash during esp_image_verify() makes QEMU
            abort. With this option a high-priority task spins in IRAM on the other core
            for those calls. Not needed (and not wanted) on the chip: there it would stop
            core 1, where the RS485 bus owner runs, during a real update. So it is only
            offered in the emulator build (GW_IO_SIMULATED).

endmenu
